From 752b8b72635efdc79d1a0350c19133a75686ff54 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 28 Oct 2026 23:25:15 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] VideoPlayer: an HEVC stream that goes on after its decoder
 was drained loses no picture

In the hardware test of 1.0 RC4, after a seek over a slow network, the log said
"DV FEL: resynchronizing the enhancement layer (unknown)" and 19 presentations
went out without the FEL residual. What happens there, read in the code and
reproduced on the host with FFmpeg 9.0.2:

- When no packet comes for ten frame periods, the video thread takes the
  stream for a still frame and drains the decoder (DVD_CODEC_CTRL_DRAIN): it is
  sent its end so that the pictures it holds come out. A decoder that has been
  sent its end takes no packet until it is flushed (avcodec_send_packet()
  answers AVERROR_EOF), and once flushed it has none of the pictures that the
  next packets refer to: FFmpeg's HEVC decoder gives no picture until the next
  random-access point. So a stream that had only run dry lost the packets that
  came while the decoder still gave its pictures, and every picture after them
  until its next random-access point (the video stood still), in Kodi as
  published too.
- With Dolby Vision FEL, the enhancement layer, drained with the base layer,
  refused the first base packet after the drain, and the resynchronization
  that followed threw away the enhancement pictures it had decoded for the
  base pictures still to come out: those went out without the residual, with
  the metadata of their own access units (the 13 of the log).

Now (CDrainRestart, DrainRestart.h): the decoder of an HEVC stream keeps the
packets from its last random-access point on (an IRAP picture, which the
packet says; 600 packets and 128 MiB at most). A packet that comes once the
decoder has been sent its end is not taken until the decoder has given every
picture it held (the player gives it again, as on EAGAIN; 3 s at most). Then
the decoder starts again at that random-access point: it is reset as after a
seek to it, with the enhancement layer, which starts again at the same packet
by its own rules (by picture order or by the times of two tracks), it is given
the kept packets again, one at a time as it takes them, and their pictures,
which went out before, are left out, by their time. The packet that came
follows, and the stream goes on as if it had not run dry. The enhancement
layer is drained only once the base decoder has been sent its end, not as soon
as the drain is asked for, so that a base layer that goes on is not left with
a drained layer. Without a random-access point kept (too many packets since
it, a packet without a time), the decoder starts again at the packet that
came, as before, and the log says so and why.

kodi.log, without debugging: one line for each restart ("the stream goes on
after its decoder was drained ...; the decoder starts again at its
random-access point of T s, with the N packets since then given again ..."),
and, for FEL, one line for each run of base pictures that went out without
their enhancement picture: how many, from when, what ended the run (a
pairing, a reset such as a seek, a resynchronization, the end of the video,
MEL) and why it began (the reason of the resynchronization, a seek before the
first random-access picture the layer can start at, late pictures, a drain).
Before, a run was said only when it ended with a paired picture, and a reset
in between (the restart after a drain, a seek) took its count away: the log of
1.0 RC4 had no such line.

Host test (fel_drain_test, with the module of the tree and the glue modelled
on this file, the base layer decoded in software through a pipeline that gives
a picture once it holds two, as VAAPI does), each case against a run of the
same packets without the drain. Nine cases: BL_EL.mkv with a drain in the
middle of a group of pictures (whole, and stopped after two pictures, which
leaves the decoder draining when the packets come again), three packets after
a seek, right after the random-access packet, with start codes, without a
pipeline, and once the layer has started; one_track.mkv; one_track_swap.mkv,
whose base layer reorders its pictures. With the code before, all nine fail:
4 to 21 pictures lost (the seek: 21) and 5 to 16 paired without the drain and
not with it. With this code: none lost or twice, every picture paired as
without the drain, no resynchronization, but for what no restart can give
back: in streams whose layers are coded in different picture order, 1 picture
that the drain gave out before its enhancement access unit had come (two
cases); in the stream whose base layer reorders, 2 pictures presented before
the last one the drain gave out, which are left out (they could not go out in
their order). Four mutations of DrainRestart.h (no waiting, no leaving out,
the restart at the last packet, no restart) each make a case fail.

Not run in Kodi; not seen on hardware.
